# The goonix secrets vault

This is the specification for the in-tree vault. It fixes the file layout,
the data grammar and the threat model before the implementation.

## Layout

A project has one shared vault in `secrets/`:

```
secrets/
  vault.scm
  wireguard-key.age
  root_ed25519.age
  root_ed25519.pub
```

There is one ASCII-armored age file per secret.  A public half is an ordinary
tracked file and is not secret.  A local vault uses the same layout in an untracked directory chosen by the
caller. A name in both the shared and local vault is an error. The command
writes public facts of a local vault to `flake.local.scm` as local options,
never to tracked project files. The public half of key `NAME` is the local
option `NAME-public-key`, whose value is the public key text.

`vault.scm` is data.  Reading it evaluates no project code, reads no
identity, accesses no network and performs no decryption.

## Grammar

The file contains exactly one `vault` form.  Its members may appear in any
order; duplicate members, names or recipients are errors.  Unknown members
and trailing forms are errors.  Names are Scheme symbols matching
`[a-zA-Z0-9][a-zA-Z0-9_.-]*`.  Strings are UTF-8.

```scheme
(vault
 (format 1)
 (recipients
  (wrath "age1…")
  (laptop "ssh-ed25519 AAAA…")
  (hurd64 "age1…"))
 (secrets
  (root_ed25519 (kind ssh-key) (for wrath))
  (wireguard-key (kind file) (for wrath hurd64)
                 (owner "root") (mode #o400))))
```

`(format 1)` is required.  `recipients` and `secrets` are required, though
their lists may be empty.  A recipient value is either an age X25519 public
key or an SSH Ed25519 public key.  `kind` is one of `file`, `password` or
`ssh-key`; the default is `file`.  `for` is a nonempty list of recipient
names declared in this vault. The age file has exactly one stanza for each
recipient named in `for`, and no other.

The ciphertext path is `NAME.age`, relative to the vault directory. The
public half of an `ssh-key` is `NAME.pub` in the same directory.  `owner` is a user
name and defaults to `root`; `group` is an optional group name.  `mode` is
an integer permission mask from 0 through `#o777`, default `#o400`.  These
fields describe materialisation on a machine; they never change ciphertext
or a derivation.

An identity is outside the project and the store.  A person's default is
`~/.config/guix/secrets/identity.txt`; a machine's default is
`/etc/guix/secrets/identity.txt`, mode 600.  An SSH private key may be named
explicitly instead. A machine may use its SSH host key as its identity. The
implementation must not copy either kind into a store item.

## Age subset

Files use age version 1, ASCII armor, and X25519 or `ssh-ed25519` recipient
stanzas. The implementation reads the standard scrypt stanza when it is
needed for an encrypted identity, but never creates a new scrypt recipient
or passphrase-protected identity. Plugins, passphrases as vault recipients,
and other age extensions are outside this implementation.

The file key is random for every encryption.  Every declared recipient gets
a stanza.  The header MAC covers the canonical header; payload chunks use
the age STREAM construction and its final-chunk marker.  Wrong identities,
bad MACs, truncated armor, truncated payloads, modified nonces and flipped
ciphertext bits are errors.  No unauthenticated plaintext is returned.

All encryption and decryption is Scheme over libgcrypt.  Small bindings
provide the X25519 and ChaCha20-Poly1305 interfaces not exposed by
guile-gcrypt.  HKDF, HMAC and the format code are tested against the age
project's published vectors and against the stock `age` tool in both
directions.

## Purity and local options

`(secret in 'NAME)` lowers to `/run/secrets/NAME`, not to contents.
`(secret-public in 'NAME)` is the tracked public half.  Ciphertext may be
in the store because it remains encrypted.  Neither evaluation nor a build
may read an identity or decrypt a secret.  Two evaluations with different
identities must produce identical derivations.

A public fact about one machine, such as its signing key, is a local option
in `flake.local.scm`.  It participates in the cache key, remains outside
the repository, and is not treated as a secret.  Commands that generate a
local SSH key write its public half there.

## Threat model and non-promises

The store, substitutes, project history and project readers are not trusted
with plaintext. Secret names, recipients' public keys and who may read what
are public: `vault.scm` is in clear in the repository and in the store.
A recipient identity is trusted to read every past and future file encrypted
to it. Root on a machine can read that machine's plaintext and identities.
`/run/secrets` is a tmpfs; activation materialises only declared files with
their owner, group and mode.

`check` verifies that each file has exactly the stanzas of its declared
recipients, that ssh-ed25519 stanza tags match their keys, and that the
caller's identity opens the file. A stanza for an unlisted recipient is an
error. Without the other recipients' identities, `check` cannot decrypt as
them.

Age does not authenticate the sender and does not provide forward secrecy.
Rekeying protects subsequent files but does not rotate a leaked value or
erase history.  A service that copies a secret into its own store item
publishes it; the vault can provide a safe path, not prevent that misuse.
Private repository credentials and age hardware-key plugins are outside this
specification.

## Example: the guests of hurdkit-guix

The shared project contains no generated guest private key. For local
throwaway guest logins, the user runs:

```sh
guix secrets generate --local ssh-key root_ed25519
guix secrets generate --local ssh-key peon_ed25519
```

The commands write encrypted private keys in the local vault and their public
halves as the local options `root_ed25519-public-key` and
`peon_ed25519-public-key`. The system uses those options instead of reading
`img/keys/*.pub`. The host's public Guix signing key is another local option,
`host-signing-key`. The test harness starts with the vault's temporary
directory as its key directory:

```sh
guix secrets exec -- sh -c 'SSH_KEY_DIR=$GUIX_SECRETS_DIRECTORY scripts/guix_vm.sh ssh'
```

The calling shell does not expand `GUIX_SECRETS_DIRECTORY`: the command that
`exec` starts receives it. The temporary directory is removed on success,
failure or signal.
